digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56751-8
  rankdir="TB";
  "IMP_ML_Eff_Conc" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: ML/BO is effective for protein optimization."];
  "IMP_ML_Samp_Prob" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Conclusion: ML application is bottlenecked by sampling difficulties."];
  "IMP_ML_Samp_Rule" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Rule: ML efficiency depends on informative sampling strategies."];
  "IMP_TM_Know_Rule" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Rule: Targeted methods require deep structure-function knowledge."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes an integrated protein-engineering strategy in which a protein language model predicts high-fitness mutants, with or without predefined mutation sites, and a fully automated biofoundry executes a closed-loop Design-Build-Test-Learn workflow for rapid directed evolution, exemplified by iterative improvement of Methanocaldococcus jannaschii p-cyanophenylalanine tRNA synthetase.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A computational Design-Build-Test-Learn strategy can pair model-based mutant selection with automated construction and testing to accelerate iterative protein engineering."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Proteins are broadly important, and proteins often require engineering when industrial applications need enhanced properties."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Smart-library and software-guided rational-design tools can improve engineering efficiency, but Rosetta-like software tools are reported as effective mainly for relatively simple reactions."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Applying machine learning in protein engineering is challenged by data acquisition, data modeling, and the time- and labor-intensive collection of functional data."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-Conclusion: A fully integrated biofoundry can be understood as an automated platform combining high-throughput instruments and peripheral devices coordinated by robotic arms and scheduling software."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: The SAMPLE platform was an autonomous protein-engineering platform that explored a small protein landscape of 1352 sequences."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: A reported BO-guided robotic evolutionary algorithm explored a four-site combinatorial library, but that BO algorithm could not guide mutant residue selection and its generality remained unknown."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Traditional directed evolution has practical limitations including time/labor demands and possible trapping in local fitness optima."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Traditional directed evolution relies on iterative random mutagenesis and screening and is described as time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: In the reported PLMeAE experiments, four rounds completed within about half a month progressively improved enzyme activity and outperformed random selection and traditional directed evolution."];
  "R34" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Protein language models learn protein structure-function principles from evolutionary sequence data and have been useful for modeling functional proteins and designing novel proteins."];
  "R35" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Prior PLM-guided optimization examples include improved antibody binding affinity and assisted optimization of uracil-N-glycosylase variant activity."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-Conclusion: Directed evolution can be limited by trapping in local fitness optima during iterative improvement."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Conclusion: Targeted mutagenesis depends on appropriate target-site choice and structure-function understanding."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Proteins play a vital role in various fields."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Success of targeted mutagenesis depends on target site choice and structure-function understanding."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Software tools like Rosetta are effective only for relatively simple reactions."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Iterative BO demonstrated effectiveness in enhancing thermostability of a P450 enzyme."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Similar methods applied to engineer activity of halogenase, enantioselectivity, and fluorescence."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Widespread application of ML in protein engineering remains challenging in acquiring and modeling data."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Proteins often require engineering to enhance properties for industrial applications."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) Experimentally collecting functional data is time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) It is difficult to efficiently sample informative mutants without prior structure-function information."];
  "S22" [label="(22,0,0) Protein language models learned fundamental principles of protein structure and function from evolutionary data."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) PLMs have proven powerful in modeling functional proteins and designing novel proteins."];
  "S25" [label="(25,0,0) Affinity maturation of antibodies guided by PLMs improved binding affinities up to 160-fold."];
  "S26" [label="(26,0,0) PLMs assisted optimization of uracil-N-glycosylase variant activity for base editing."];
  "S29" [label="(29,0,0) A fully integrated biofoundry combines high-throughput core instruments and peripheral devices."];
  "S30" [label="(30,0,0) Biofoundry components are seamlessly coordinated by robotic arms and scheduling software."];
  "S35" [label="(35,0,0) SAMPLE platform was developed for fully autonomous protein engineering."];
  "S36" [label="(36,0,0) SAMPLE platform only explored a small protein landscape containing 1352 protein sequences."];
  "S37" [label="(37,0,0) BO-guided evolutionary algorithm with robotic experiments explored a four-site combinatorial library."];
  "S38" [label="(38,0,0) BO algorithm could not guide mutant residue selection, and generality remains unknown."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Traditional directed evolution relies on iterative cycles of random mutagenesis and screening."];
  "S46" [label="(46,0,0) Four rounds performed within half a month with enzyme activity progressively improving."];
  "S47" [label="(47,0,0) PLMeAE system exhibited superior performance compared to random selection and traditional directed evolution."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) The process of traditional directed evolution is time-consuming and labor-intensive."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Directed evolution can be trapped in local fitness optima, limiting further improvement."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Targeted mutagenesis guided by structure produces small-but-smart libraries."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Smart libraries improve the efficiency of directed evolution."];
